WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.000
Delegation needs change control, and the
useful pattern is an approval workflow.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.125
A division's group submits a change,
with a ticket number required.

3
00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:13.287
A central approver group approves,
rejects or reschedules it.

4
00:00:13.287 --> 00:00:17.944
The task then runs with the submitter's
own permissions, so approval can't grant

5
00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:19.878
rights the division doesn't have,

6
00:00:19.878 --> 00:00:22.887
and it lands in the audit
log with a task identifier.

7
00:00:22.887 --> 00:00:28.624
That's NIOS; a Universal DDI
equivalent wasn't confirmed, so ask.

8
00:00:28.624 --> 00:00:31.800
The weakness of any workflow
is what goes around it.

9
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.747
In NIOS, CSV imports don't require approval
at all, and the console warns when

10
00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:42.013
a submitter group holds import permission;
remove it from every division group.

11
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:45.038
Service restarts aren't
subject to approval either.

12
00:00:45.038 --> 00:00:50.137
Some operations are blocked for submitters
even when they hold the permission:

13
00:00:50.137 --> 00:00:53.613
signing or unsigning zones,
key-signing key rollovers,

14
00:00:53.613 --> 00:00:56.626
resizing or splitting
networks, and bulk edits.

15
00:00:56.626 --> 00:01:01.555
And a zone delegated to a division's own
servers leaves the platform entirely:

16
00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:04.637
its changes, its response
policy and its signing.

17
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:07.350
So decide what stays
central in every pattern.

18
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:12.063
The superuser accounts, and the
definitions of groups, roles and tags.

19
00:01:12.063 --> 00:01:15.312
The approval workflows and
the authentication policy.

20
00:01:15.312 --> 00:01:17.262
DNSSEC keys and signing.

21
00:01:17.262 --> 00:01:21.726
Security policy, meaning response
policy zones and threat feeds.

22
00:01:21.726 --> 00:01:23.612
The top-level address blocks.

23
00:01:23.612 --> 00:01:28.220
And logging, with the platform-wide
DNS and DHCP settings.
